@Andrzej I’ve heard of Ohio as the Buckeye state but had no idea what that was. Now thanks to your Polish translation I discover that it’s related to the chestnut, which is châtaigne in French (marrons are big ones, used to make marrons glacés) and castaño in Spanish, which is where the musical instrument (castanets) comes from. 

In Britain schoolkids (used to) play a game called conkers, in which you have a chestnut on a string (through a hole drilled in the middle) and you take turns trying to hit the other person’s dangling conker until one of them breaks. 

So the Spanish make music, the French make food, the English play games, the Americans make a great State.

@Hitch 

"Grok" comes from  Robert A. Heinlein's novel "Stranger in a Strange Land." The best description of its meaning I can come up with is to grasp the essense of something completely, from every angle and on every level.

People have taken up using it online, so when it's encountered by someone who never read the book they usually infer that it's just a synonym for "understand," so the meaning has, as usual,  shifted with use towards something less precise. That process is inevitable, I guess, but it doesn't make me happy.

